# Append-only JSONL history logs. Concurrent producers on `main` (deploy
# shards + crons) only ever append rows, so a line-union merge is always the
# correct resolution. Without this, a rebase against a remote that gained new
# rows hand-conflicts, the deploy retry loop is left mid-rebase, and every
# retry then dies on "Pulling is not possible because you have unmerged files"
# until the job is canceled (run 27976954013: dist-size-history.jsonl conflict
# wedged the IT deploy shard). `merge=union` keeps both sides' new lines so the
# conflict cannot occur by construction.
data/dist-size-history.jsonl       merge=union
data/quality-alerts-history.jsonl  merge=union
data/quota-history.jsonl           merge=union
data/thin-page-promotions.jsonl    merge=union
# data/prospector/ledger.jsonl e' scritto in append da DUE lati nello stesso
# giro: sul branch della PR di promozione (durante lo scaffold) e su main (dal
# commit di stato). Senza `merge=union` le due code collidono e wedgiano
# esattamente il merge che deve avvenire da solo.
data/prospector/ledger.jsonl       merge=union

# data/article-defect-history.jsonl is the article pipeline's rejection ledger
# (docs/ARTICLE-LEARNING-LOOP.md §4.2): one row per generate-article run, saying
# what rejected it and what it cost. generate-article.yml serializes itself
# (`concurrency: article-generation`, `cancel-in-progress: false`), so it has a
# single producer TODAY — but it is committed on the same push that races every
# other main writer, and the union driver is what keeps a future second producer
# from wedging the rebase-retry loop the way dist-size-history.jsonl once did.
# Registering it now costs one line; discovering it is missing costs a stuck
# push during an incident.
#
# The writer applies a 180-day / 12000-row retention IN the append call, which
# rewrites the file and therefore does not commute with a line-union merge: a
# union can resurrect rows a compaction just dropped. That is deliberate and
# harmless — rows are identified by `runId|at`, the reader deduplicates on that
# key so a resurrected row cannot be counted twice, and the next append drops it
# again. Convergent, no coordination needed.
data/article-defect-history.jsonl  merge=union

# data/url-first-seen.json is a pretty-printed JSON OBJECT ("url": "ISO-date",
# one sorted line each), not a JSONL log — so `merge=union` cannot be used (it
# would concatenate both sides' lines into duplicate keys / invalid JSON). The
# same multi-producer race that the union driver fixes for the logs above
# wedged the dist-history push loop on this file instead (run 28142157430: 5
# rebase attempts, all CONFLICT → aborted → never landed). The custom
# `json-first-seen` driver unions the two maps keeping the EARLIEST first-seen
# date per URL (monotonic), so the conflict cannot occur by construction. The
# driver is defined with `git config merge.json-first-seen.driver` in the
# workflow step that rebases this file (see .github/workflows/deploy.yml).
data/url-first-seen.json           merge=json-first-seen

# data/seo-404-compat/part-*.json are the shards of the seo-404 compat store
# (issue #2988 split the >100MB monolith into 16 sorted JSON shards). Each shard
# is `{ "paths": [sorted strings] }`. Many producers rewrite them concurrently;
# writeCompatPaths sorts, so git's default line merge keeps both rewritten
# versions → ~2x duplicate, unsorted shards (the store dedups on read, but the
# on-disk bloat is real). `merge=union` would only add more duplicates. The
# custom `compat-shard` driver does a 3-way SET merge → deduped + sorted by
# construction. Driver wired via `git config merge.compat-shard.driver` in
# scripts/lib/git-push-with-retry.sh and scripts/lib/git-commit-data.sh.
data/seo-404-compat/part-*.json merge=compat-shard

# data/all-known-job-slugs/part-*.json are the shards of the canonical slug
# registry (issue #4248 split the >100MB monolith into 32 sorted JSON shards).
# Each shard is `{ "slugs": { "<slug>": {it,en,de,fr} } }` with sorted keys.
# Same store shape, same concurrent producers and same failure mode as the
# seo-404 compat shards above — sync-gsc-orphans, mine-all-job-slugs and
# migrate-all-known-job-slugs-canton-aware all re-serialise the whole sorted
# shard, so git's default line merge has no stable anchor and either conflicts
# (wedging the push-retry loop) or keeps both rewrites, which for a JSON OBJECT
# means DUPLICATE KEYS. `merge=union` would only add more. The custom
# `known-slugs-shard` driver does a 3-way MAP merge → sorted + canonical by
# construction. Driver wired via `git config merge.known-slugs-shard.driver` in
# scripts/lib/git-push-with-retry.sh and scripts/lib/git-commit-data.sh.
data/all-known-job-slugs/part-*.json merge=known-slugs-shard

# data/orphan-enriched-data/part-*.json are the shards of the enriched-orphan
# ledger (issue #4248 split the >100MB monolith into 32 sorted JSON shards).
# Each shard is `{ "orphans": [ {slug, locale, queries, …} ] }` sorted by
# (slug, locale). Same store family, same concurrent producers and same failure
# mode as the two shard sets above — sync-gsc-orphans rewrites the whole ledger
# every run and enrich-compat-orphan-slugs appends to it, so git's default line
# merge has no stable anchor and either conflicts (wedging the push-retry loop)
# or keeps both rewrites, which for a JSON ARRAY means DUPLICATE RECORDS and a
# doubled blob — re-creating the very size problem the shard exists to solve.
# The custom `orphan-enriched-shard` driver does a 3-way merge on the record set
# keyed by (locale, slug) → deduped + sorted by construction. Driver wired via
# `git config merge.orphan-enriched-shard.driver` in
# scripts/lib/git-push-with-retry.sh and scripts/lib/git-commit-data.sh.
data/orphan-enriched-data/part-*.json merge=orphan-enriched-shard

# data/jobs-ai-cache.json is the crawlers' persistent LLM response cache. Unlike
# the three shard sets above it is NOT sharded, because it is a CACHE and not an
# accumulator: it is bounded by a byte budget instead (issue #4248 follow-up,
# scripts/lib/ai-cache-budget.mjs), since GitHub's 100 MB push limit is in bytes
# while the pre-existing cap counted entries — and entry size here spans two
# orders of magnitude, so the count cap permitted a ~104 MB file.
#
# The budget alone is not enough. ~40 crawlers write this file concurrently, and
# git's generic 3-way merge keys the `entries` array on `key` and UNIONS both
# sides — a union of two sides each exactly at budget is ABOVE budget, so the
# merge would silently undo the bound the persist step just applied and the
# whole crawler group's push would die on GH001. The custom `ai-cache` driver
# unions by key keeping the newest touchedAt, then re-applies the byte budget,
# so the merged result is bounded by construction. Driver wired via
# `git config merge.ai-cache.driver` in scripts/lib/git-push-with-retry.sh and
# scripts/lib/git-commit-data.sh.
data/jobs-ai-cache.json merge=ai-cache
